  "question": {
    "text": "A bar of mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>00</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>kg</mi></math> and length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math> is lying on a horizontal frictionless surface. One end of the bar is pivoted at a point about which it is free to rotate. A small mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>kg</mi></math> is moving on the same horizontal surface with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>00</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>&#8211;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math> speed on a path perpendicular to the bar. It hits the bar at a distance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math> from the pivoted end and returns back on the same path with speed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>v</mi></math>. After this elastic collision, the bar rotates with an angular velocity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#969;</mi></math>. Which of the following statement is correct?",
    "image": null
  },
